import pf.Html
import ../../../Ui/Heading
import ../../../Ui/Code
import ../../../Generated/Sources
import ../../../Generated/Paths
import ../../../Ui/PageHero
import ../../../Ui/TableWrap

## The posthog package: an app's errors sent to PostHog's error tracking, from the app itself (a
## middleware, a plugin's routes and loop), grouped by fingerprints of its own; and the daily digest
## that reads them. Static: PostHog is a server.
meta = {
	title: "PostHog",
	description: "packages/posthog, a plugin for Roc Solid's server: 5xx answers, errors the app deals with, jobs that failed for good and errors in the browser, sent to PostHog's error tracking, grouped into issues, with a daily digest that reads them.",
	toc: [
		{ id: "install", label: "Installing it" },
		{ id: "what", label: "What it reports" },
		{ id: "capture", label: "An error the app handles" },
		{ id: "where", label: "Where they go" },
		{ id: "issues", label: "Issues" },
		{ id: "digest", label: "A daily digest" },
		{ id: "limits", label: "Not reported" },
	],
}

view! = |_|
	<>
		<NoHydration>
			<PageHero
				kicker={[<>{"The ecosystem · "}</>, <code>packages/posthog</code>]}
				title="Errors in PostHog, grouped into issues, from the app itself"
			>
				Every 5xx answer, every error the app handles but wants seen, every job that failed for good, and every
				uncaught error in a browser, sent to <a href="https://posthog.com/docs/error-tracking">PostHog's error
				tracking</a> with where it happened and who it reached.
			</PageHero>
			<p>
				Use this package when you want to hear about an app's errors in production without reading its log.
				It is a <a href={"${Paths.platform_server}#plugins"}>plugin</a> plus a
				<a href={"${Paths.routing}#middleware"}>middleware</a>, written in Roc on top of what any plugin can do:
				the platform has no error-reporting hook of its own. The middleware notices failed answers, the
				plugin's routes take errors from the browser, and a background loop sends everything to PostHog in
				batches, so a request never waits on PostHog.
			</p>

			<Heading id="install">Installing it</Heading>
			<p>
				Name the package in the app's header (<code>{"posthog: \"…/packages/posthog/main.roc\""}</code>), then
				wire it into the program in three places:
			</p>
			<Code title={"${Sources.posthog_program.file}, in part"} source={Sources.posthog_program.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<Code title={Sources.posthog_middleware.file} source={Sources.posthog_middleware.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<ul>
				<li>
					<strong>The middleware</strong>, <code>PostHog.middleware(user)</code>, goes in
					<code>routes/+middleware.roc</code>, after the middleware that knows who is asking. <code>user</code> is a function from that middleware's context to the person's
					id. Return <code>""</code> when nobody is known; with no such middleware, put PostHog's first, with
					<code>{"|_| \"\""}</code>.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>The plugin</strong>, <code>{"PostHog.plugin({})"}</code>, goes in the app's plugins. It reads
					its key from the environment (see <a href="#where">where they go</a>).
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Failed jobs</strong> are reported when the <a href={Paths.ecosystem_jobs}>jobs</a> plugin is
					given <code>{"on_failed!: PostHog.job_failed!"}</code>.
				</li>
			</ul>
			<p>
				For errors in the browser, add one line to the layout:
				<code>{"<script src=\"/rocx-errors.js\" defer></script>"}</code>. The plugin serves that script.
			</p>

			<Heading id="what">What it reports</Heading>
			<TableWrap>
				<table>
					<thead><tr><th>What</th><th>How</th><th>Type in PostHog</th><th>Handled</th></tr></thead>
					<tbody>
						<tr><td>an answer with a 5xx status</td><td><code>PostHog.middleware(user)</code></td><td><code>ServerError</code></td><td>no</td></tr>
						<tr><td>an error the app dealt with</td><td><code>PostHog.capture!(message, fields)</code></td><td><code>Error</code></td><td>yes</td></tr>
						<tr><td>a job that failed for good</td><td><code>on_failed!: PostHog.job_failed!</code></td><td><code>JobFailed</code></td><td>no</td></tr>
						<tr><td>an error or a rejected promise nothing caught in a browser</td><td><code>/rocx-errors.js</code></td><td>the error's own (<code>TypeError</code>)</td><td>no</td></tr>
					</tbody>
				</table>
			</TableWrap>
			<p>
				A 5xx report carries the route's pattern (<code>/posts/[id]</code>, not the path with its ids), the
				method, the status, the first line of the answer's text (unless the answer is HTML), and who asked. An
				error from nobody known creates no person in PostHog.
			</p>
			<p>
				Each error is first kept in the server's memory (<a href={Paths.platform_kv}>Kv</a>, the newest 1,000).
				A background loop sends what is kept every ten seconds, and once more as the server stops, as
				<code>$exception</code> events to PostHog's batch endpoint.
			</p>

			<Heading id="capture">An error the app handles</Heading>
			<p>
				Most of what goes wrong in a Roc app is a value, not a crash: a refusal, a database error, a fetch that
				timed out. When the app handles one but still wants it seen, it calls <code>PostHog.capture!</code>:
			</p>
			<Code title={"${Sources.posthog_capture.file}, in part"} source={Sources.posthog_capture.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<p>
				<code>capture!</code> takes a message and a record of fields, as <code>Log.error!</code> does. When it is
				called while answering a request, the report also carries that request's path and method.
				<code>{"PostHog.capture_for!(user, message, fields)"}</code> does the same and says which person it
				reached, so PostHog can count the people an issue affects.
			</p>

			<Heading id="where">Where they go</Heading>
			<p>
				Each option can be given to <code>{"PostHog.plugin({ … })"}</code> or, for the first three, set in the
				environment. An option given in code wins.
			</p>
			<TableWrap>
				<table>
					<thead><tr><th>Option</th><th>Environment</th><th>Default</th></tr></thead>
					<tbody>
						<tr><td><code>key</code></td><td><code>POSTHOG_KEY</code></td><td>none: reporting is off</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>host</code></td><td><code>POSTHOG_HOST</code></td><td><code>https://us.i.posthog.com</code> (use <code>https://eu.i.posthog.com</code> for EU Cloud)</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>release</code></td><td><code>POSTHOG_RELEASE</code></td><td>none; set it to the commit a build is from</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>at</code></td><td></td><td><code>/rocx-errors</code> (its script is <code>/rocx-errors.js</code>)</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>interval_ms</code></td><td></td><td><code>10000</code></td></tr>
					</tbody>
				</table>
			</TableWrap>
			<p>
				Without a key nothing is kept or sent, which is what you want while developing. Once the server has
				started, it logs where errors go, or that reporting is off.
			</p>

			<Heading id="issues">Issues</Heading>
			<p>
				PostHog groups errors into issues, usually by their stack trace. Roc has no stack traces, so the package
				tells PostHog which issue each error belongs to (its <code>$exception_fingerprint</code>):
			</p>
			<ul>
				<li>a 5xx answer, by its route's pattern and its message;</li>
				<li>a captured error, by its message alone, so it is one issue wherever it happens;</li>
				<li>a failed job, by its kind and its error;</li>
				<li>a browser's error, by its type and message.</li>
			</ul>
			<p>
				Parts that change from one occurrence to the next are taken out first. A word with a digit in it counts
				as any other, so <code>user 1234 not found</code> and <code>user 77 not found</code> are one issue, and
				so does text in double quotes or backticks.
			</p>
			<p>
				So that a loop of errors can't flood the project, one issue is sent at most 20 times a minute, and all
				browsers together send at most 120 errors a minute. If PostHog doesn't take a batch (it answers 5xx, or
				doesn't answer), the batch is sent again next time. If it refuses one (a 4xx, such as a wrong key), the
				batch is dropped and the log says so.
			</p>

			<Heading id="digest">A daily digest</Heading>
			<p>
				Errors are worth sending somewhere only if something reads them. PostHog's MCP server lists the issues
				and how many people each reached. A scheduled agent can read it once a day, write a digest of what is
				new or came back, and for each top issue: check out its <code>release</code>, write a failing test from
				its route, method and fields, fix the bug on a branch and open a pull request for a person to review.
				The <a href="https://github.com/Roc-Solid/rocx/tree/master/packages/posthog">package's README</a> has
				the steps.
			</p>

			<Heading id="limits">Not reported</Heading>
			<ul>
				<li>
					<strong>A crash in Roc while answering a request.</strong> It ends the call before the middleware sees
					an answer. The host answers 500 and logs <code>rocx server: Roc crashed</code>, so crashes are on the
					server's log, not in PostHog. (A job that crashes is reported: to the jobs plugin, a crash is a
					failure.)
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Some failures in the browser.</strong> A computation that fails with no
					<code>{"<Errored>"}</code> around it, and a handler that fails, are written to the browser's console by
					the runtime and never reach the script.
				</li>
			</ul>
		</NoHydration>
	</>
